counterfactuals.output:
A method is provided for assigning Layer 2 cellular processing tasks between a radio transceiver point and a virtual machine in a cloud or virtualized telecommunications network. The method supports dynamic functional placement of protocol processing in a radio access network in which at least a portion of baseband processing is executed by virtualized compute resources.

The method comprises receiving, at a control entity, configuration and operating information associated with a radio transceiver point and one or more virtual machines. The radio transceiver point may include radio frequency circuitry, antenna interfaces, and local processing resources configured to perform one or more cellular protocol functions. The virtual machine may be hosted on a cloud computing platform, an edge computing platform, or a centralized radio access network processing platform.

The configuration and operating information may include one or more of:

- available compute capacity at the radio transceiver point;
- available compute capacity at the virtual machine;
- fronthaul or midhaul transport latency;
- fronthaul or midhaul bandwidth;
- jitter and packet loss conditions;
- radio cell bandwidth;
- number of active user equipments;
- scheduling load;
- hybrid automatic repeat request timing constraints;
- modulation and coding requirements;
- quality-of-service requirements;
- power consumption constraints;
- fault status of processing elements;
- virtualization resource availability; and
- operator-defined placement policies.

Based on the received information, the control entity determines an assignment of Layer 2 cellular processing tasks between the radio transceiver point and the virtual machine. The Layer 2 cellular processing tasks may include one or more of medium access control processing, radio link control processing, packet data convergence protocol processing, segmentation and reassembly, scheduling assistance, buffer management, ciphering or integrity-related packet handling, header compression, retransmission control, logical channel prioritization, and control-channel related processing.

The determination may include evaluating a set of placement criteria. For example, time-critical Layer 2 functions may be assigned to the radio transceiver point when the transport latency between the radio transceiver point and the virtual machine exceeds a threshold. Conversely, computationally intensive or less latency-sensitive Layer 2 functions may be assigned to the virtual machine when adequate transport bandwidth and latency are available. The control entity may further consider whether the radio transceiver point has sufficient local processing capacity to meet radio scheduling deadlines and whether the virtual machine has sufficient compute resources to process Layer 2 traffic for one or more cells.

In one implementation, the method includes classifying each Layer 2 processing task according to at least one execution constraint. The execution constraint may include a maximum processing delay, a required processing throughput, a required memory capacity, an affinity to another processing function, or a dependency on radio timing information. The method then compares the execution constraint with current resource and transport conditions and selects an execution location for the task.

The method may further comprise generating an assignment instruction identifying, for each Layer 2 processing task or group of tasks, whether the task is to be executed at the radio transceiver point or at the virtual machine. The assignment instruction may include task identifiers, cell identifiers, bearer identifiers, user equipment identifiers, processing priority, timing parameters, and data forwarding rules. The assignment instruction may be transmitted to the radio transceiver point, the virtual machine, or both.

After assignment, user-plane and/or control-plane data associated with the assigned Layer 2 tasks is routed according to the selected functional split. Where a task is assigned to the radio transceiver point, corresponding protocol data units may be processed locally at the radio transceiver point. Where a task is assigned to the virtual machine, corresponding data may be transported over a fronthaul or midhaul interface to the virtual machine for processing, and processed data may be returned or forwarded according to the applicable radio access network architecture.

The method may also include monitoring performance after the assignment has been applied. The monitoring may include measuring processing latency, queue occupancy, packet delay variation, retransmission rate, throughput, processor utilization, and radio link performance. If a monitored parameter crosses a predetermined threshold, the control entity may modify the assignment of one or more Layer 2 tasks. Such modification may include migrating a task from the virtual machine to the radio transceiver point, migrating a task from the radio transceiver point to the virtual machine, duplicating processing for redundancy, or splitting processing among multiple virtual machines.

In some embodiments, reassignment is performed without interrupting an active radio connection. The method may include synchronizing protocol state between the current execution location and a target execution location prior to migration. The protocol state may include sequence numbers, retransmission buffers, scheduling context, bearer configuration, ciphering state, radio link control state, packet data convergence protocol state, and quality-of-service flow mapping. Upon completion of state synchronization, processing of selected Layer 2 tasks is switched to the target execution location.

The method may be implemented by a network controller, radio access network intelligent controller, orchestration system, baseband control function, or other management entity. The method may operate in conjunction with virtual network function management, container orchestration, network slicing, and service-level policy enforcement. The virtual machine may execute a virtualized Layer 2 processing function, and the radio transceiver point may execute a local Layer 2 processing function or accelerator-assisted processing function.

Accordingly, the method enables adaptive allocation of Layer 2 cellular processing between distributed radio equipment and virtualized cloud resources, thereby balancing latency, compute utilization, bandwidth consumption, reliability, and service quality in a virtualized radio access network.
